Tell the console keyboard when a field is a password.

A focused edit box calls `SDL_StartTextInput`, which on this platform opens the system
keyboard (`oops-deps/sdl2`). SDL2 has no notion of a password field, so the keyboard
showed the password as it was typed and learned it into the system dictionary. The box
now sets the backend's `SDL_OOPS_IME_PASSWORD` hint from its own `PasswordBox` first, so
the keyboard masks the entry and never learns it. Every field is kept out of the
dictionary regardless; this adds the masking.

STK marks its password fields itself (`user_screen.cpp`, `register_screen.cpp`, the
server password and change-password dialogs), so the one call site covers them all.

diff --git a/src/guiengine/widgets/CGUIEditBox.cpp b/src/guiengine/widgets/CGUIEditBox.cpp
index 865997f..3c22e2c 100644
--- a/src/guiengine/widgets/CGUIEditBox.cpp
+++ b/src/guiengine/widgets/CGUIEditBox.cpp
@@ -410,6 +410,11 @@ bool CGUIEditBox::OnEvent(const SEvent& event)
                 // In windows we use caret to determine candidate box, which
                 // needs to calculate it first
                 calculateScrollPos();
+#endif
+#ifdef OOPS_TARGET
+                // The console keyboard masks a password and never learns it
+                // (oops-deps/sdl2); SDL2 itself has no password field.
+                SDL_SetHint("SDL_OOPS_IME_PASSWORD", PasswordBox ? "1" : "0");
 #endif
                 SDL_StartTextInput();
 #endif
